function surveyPrompt(unit) {
  return `You are auditing the Callisto Rust workspace at the current working directory for spec-vs-code drift, as part of a full spec-conformance audit across all crates. This is READ-ONLY auditing -- do not edit any files.

Your assigned unit: ${unit.crate}

Read ${unit.specRange} in docs/01-spec.md. Also grep docs/00-design.md for ${unit.designHint} and read any sections that describe requirements for this unit (docs/00-design.md is the requirements/design doc; docs/01-spec.md is the technical implementation spec -- both make claims that should hold against real code).

For every distinct claim, feature, function signature, error variant, acceptance criterion, or behavioral guarantee the spec/design docs make about this unit, verify it against the ACTUAL current source code in the corresponding crate directory (crates/<crate-name>/src/). Use Read and Grep freely -- read real source files, not just what the docs say.

Classify each claim as either confirmed (implementation matches what's documented) or a gap. A gap is any of: the described feature/function/type doesn't exist at all; it exists but the signature/behavior differs from what's documented; it's documented as complete/shipped but is actually dead code, unreachable, or only partially wired (this exact pattern was found once already this session -- a CLI feature flag that compiled but was never actually consulted by any handler, so pay special attention to "is this described capability actually reachable end-to-end, not just defined"); a test the spec says must exist doesn't; or the docs and code flatly contradict each other on some fact (a field type, an error message, a citation to a section that doesn't say what's claimed).

Do not report a gap for trivial wording/paraphrasing differences in prose descriptions -- only report gaps that represent a real functional or factual discrepancy. Do not spend time on cosmetic issues.

For each gap found, provide: a one-sentence description, the exact spec citation (section number + a short quote or line reference), what the spec says should be true, what you actually found in the code (with file:line), and a severity (critical = wrong/dangerous behavior in production code; high = documented feature genuinely missing or broken; medium = real drift but low practical impact; low = minor/cosmetic-adjacent but still a real discrepancy).

Also report: roughly how many distinct claims you checked in total, and how many were confirmed correct (a rough count is fine, you don't need to enumerate every confirmation).

Be thorough -- this is a full audit, not a spot check. Read the entire assigned spec range, not just the first few sections. If your assigned unit is large, prioritize breadth (cover the whole range) over exhaustive depth on any one part.`
}

function verifyPrompt(unit, gap) {
  return `You are adversarially verifying a claimed spec-vs-code discrepancy found during a full audit of the Callisto Rust workspace at the current working directory. Your default assumption is that the claimed gap is WRONG until you can confirm it by reading the actual code and spec yourself -- a prior audit pass can be mistaken (wrong file, code has since changed, misread the spec, missed a code path that actually handles the case).

Unit: ${unit.crate}
Claimed gap: ${gap.description}
Spec citation: ${gap.spec_citation}
Spec says: ${gap.expected}
Claimed actual behavior: ${gap.actual}
Evidence cited: ${gap.evidence}

Read the cited spec section yourself. Read the cited code location yourself, plus any related code (callers, sibling implementations, tests) that might contradict the claim -- e.g. a different code path that actually provides the missing behavior, a test that already covers this, or a citation that says something different from what was quoted.

Confirm the gap is real (confirmed: true) only if you independently verify both that the spec citation says what's claimed AND that the code genuinely doesn't do what's claimed. Otherwise mark confirmed: false and explain what the original finding got wrong.`
}

